fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson) writes:

>The exact term we want, keeping to the Greek roots, is "autocracy" for
>self-rule, but that has already been co-opted to mean despotism, or rule
>of many by one. Strange ... they didn't do the same to "autoeroticism".
>It's as if someone were to maintain that "self-control" means one person
>controls everyone.

Would any linguists or etymologists like to comment on the history of this
change in common usage?

>Since we don't learn Latin and Greek in school anymore -- and too damned
>bad, too, since languages, mathematics, logic and rhetoric are still a fine
>curriculum -- we'd do well to coin chauvinistic Anglo terms like
>"self-government" instead.

Latin for "self government": Imperium Ipsum (according to my SO; she asked
me, "Of self or by self?", and I said I guess "by", since it seems more
universally true [pardon my lack of grammar] for the concept in question).
What would the Greek for it be? If it sounds good, it might make a halfway
decent meme. (The Advocates for Self-Goverment seem to be doing a good job of
promoting and defending the Anglo version.)

>Conclusion? I dunno. I like "acracy" but I don't fancy having to explain
>it without reference to "anarchy".

Richard Feynman proposed a simple test to see whether one is teaching ideas
or mere definition: "Without using the new word which you have just learned,
try to rephrase what you have just learned in your own language." A good
intellectual exercise and potential PR tool. (PS: See my mini-review of James
Gleick's book on Feynman, which I posted along with this.)


