"AB" wrote: 

>how do extropians feel about the effects of technology outrunning the
>capability of human comprehension?  suppose computers, algorithmically and
>structurally, evolve quicker than human brain enhancement technologies.
>Will we be able to handle, understand, or manage, the data that these
>machines can produce?   Especially when we start regularly using
>self-replicating, evolving , and learning algorithms.   When we stick with
>algorithms that we design oursleves, there is no danger of losing touch
>with what we create.  However, with artificial life-like algorithms,  how
>can we be sure that we'll be able to follow the processes of evolved
>structures?

>If the gap becomes too large between what our computers can process and
>what we can process, obvious difficulties arise:  who will maintain the
>programs?  regulate them?  are they completely autonomous, and do we trust
>the data they give back to us?


One point that may be a "logic flaw" here is the notion that humanity
at large is a monolith.  Obviously, it is not.  There will be some
people who will very much grasp, acknowledge, and interact with the
implications and realities generated by not only self learning, and
self adapting "intelligent machines", but many other far reaching
technical advancements, such as nanotechnology.  

There will, however, also be a much larger portion of the global
population which will never be participants in, or even aware of,
the cutting edge of futuristic technologies.   Utopian fluffy talk
sounds nice in the comfort of a pleasant academic setting isolated
from the crushing realities of basic survival that the vast majority
of the planets' population is forced to deal with on a daily basis.

I doubt very much if a starving family in Bongladesh, or countless
other areas like it, will ever care about "self replicating, evolving,
or learning algorithms".   

It is much more likely, in fact, that as other aspects of futurstic
technology become available to the select few, such as life extension,
neural enhancement, sensory enhancement, access to "intelligent
machines", and the like, the division between the techno elite, and
the general population will become ever more pronounced, and 
statistically narrow amongst an ever growing global population.

Sorry folks.  If you want to wander around in such lofty pseudo
intellectual ponderances, I suggest STONGLY that you should travel
about the world a bit.  Those ivory towers can be a bit stifling
at times.  Comprende????

Sincerely
