From: GREG NEWTON <gnewton@ozemail.com.au>
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 94 00:49:04 +1000
Subject: [#94-9-365] Functional motivation for evolution? - Penrose's "Shadow..."

From: gnewton@ozemail.com.au(Greg Newton)

It is interesting to read Paul Ciseks' article replying to Doug Cutrell, were 
he mentions the lack of a "functional motivation for evolution of such a 
mechanism"  - my understanding of evolution is that there is no "functional 
motivation" required, as this implies the existence of a goal or a motivated 
party (a creator). Evolution is a "dumb" process of mutations being naturally 
selected by the environment at the time the mutation occurs.  Therefore *any* 
possible mutation can occur and whether it continues or not depends on the 
local environmental conditions from that time onwards.  Also I fail to see that 
primitive organisms (without consciousness) can "solve problems in order to 
survive" - they can only rely on mutations (which cannot be induced 
"artificially" by creatures without conciousness), natural selection makes the 
rules, and determines who/what wins the game.

Regards, Greg Newton
